WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:10.000
[MUSIC]

00:00:11.567 --> 00:00:14.796
A lot of my work is around
corporate income shifting,

00:00:14.796 --> 00:00:19.040
which is one way that companies
respond to tax incentives globally.

00:00:19.040 --> 00:00:24.822
So if you think of a company that has all
of its income across all of its operations

00:00:24.822 --> 00:00:30.450
around the world, it's going to be taxed
on that income in each jurisdiction.

00:00:30.450 --> 00:00:34.379
And so if there are different tax
rates in different jurisdictions,

00:00:34.379 --> 00:00:38.780
which we know there are, then it's in
the best interest of a company from a tax

00:00:38.780 --> 00:00:43.456
planning perspective to try to report
more profits in low-tax jurisdictions and

00:00:43.456 --> 00:00:45.922
less profits and high tax jurisdictions.

00:00:45.922 --> 00:00:51.416
[MUSIC]

00:00:51.416 --> 00:00:55.450
I like to think of it as, first and
foremost, an operational decision.

00:00:55.450 --> 00:00:59.905
So where as a company do you put
your footprint around the world

00:00:59.905 --> 00:01:04.095
such that you're creating value
in different jurisdictions.

00:01:04.095 --> 00:01:07.015
And this is something that gets a lot
of attention in particular with

00:01:07.015 --> 00:01:10.150
intellectual property,
patents, and things like that.

00:01:10.150 --> 00:01:14.830
Putting those in low-tax jurisdictions,
sometimes called tax havens.

00:01:14.830 --> 00:01:17.850
And that allows a lot
of the profits to move,

00:01:17.850 --> 00:01:20.240
be reported in those
low-tax jurisdictions.

00:01:20.240 --> 00:01:23.600
But it's a broader analysis than
just intellectual property.

00:01:23.600 --> 00:01:26.743
It's all of the functions, risks,
and assets of the company.

00:01:26.743 --> 00:01:31.490
And so the operational decision is
figuring out a tax efficient footprint.

00:01:31.490 --> 00:01:35.650
And then on top of that,
you can layer an accounting decision.

00:01:35.650 --> 00:01:39.700
And that is, now that I put
some patents in say, Ireland,

00:01:39.700 --> 00:01:44.620
how much of a royalty do all of the other
parts of the company have to pay

00:01:44.620 --> 00:01:49.990
to my Irish subsidiary for the right
to exploit that intellectual property?

00:01:49.990 --> 00:01:53.950
And that fundamentally,
it's an economic question, but

00:01:53.950 --> 00:01:58.700
it gets into the accounting
space of exactly how we want

00:01:58.700 --> 00:02:03.500
to assign a price, called a transfer
price, between these two subsidiaries.

00:02:03.500 --> 00:02:07.730
And where does that allow us to report
our profits from a tax perspective?

00:02:07.730 --> 00:02:13.544
[MUSIC]

00:02:13.544 --> 00:02:17.544
There's a global set of rules, and each
country has its own set of rules too for

00:02:17.544 --> 00:02:20.880
how you're supposed to come
up with what that price is.

00:02:20.880 --> 00:02:25.215
And the fundamental principle that we
operate in globally, not all countries,

00:02:25.215 --> 00:02:28.737
but most countries these days is
called the Arms Link Standards.

00:02:28.737 --> 00:02:33.409
So it's supposed to be a price that
you would observe out on the open

00:02:33.409 --> 00:02:37.630
market between two completely
unrelated parties.

00:02:37.630 --> 00:02:42.660
So if you and I were transacting for
the sale of shoes or whatever it is right,

00:02:42.660 --> 00:02:46.570
that would be the price as long as
it's at a similar level of the market.

00:02:46.570 --> 00:02:50.507
So wholesale versus retail
in a similar economy.

00:02:50.507 --> 00:02:55.043
You can take into account nuances like,
developing economies and

00:02:55.043 --> 00:02:58.230
foreign exchange rates and
things like that.

00:02:58.230 --> 00:03:00.857
But it's supposed to be as if
we're operating at arm's length.

00:03:00.857 --> 00:03:05.800
[MUSIC]

00:03:05.800 --> 00:03:09.566
The problem with that Is that
a lot of the services and

00:03:09.566 --> 00:03:15.218
products that companies are transacting
with amongst their own subsidiaries

00:03:15.218 --> 00:03:20.440
are not the same types of things
that unrelated parties transact.

00:03:20.440 --> 00:03:25.150
So it's not often the case that
a company that exploits its

00:03:25.150 --> 00:03:27.180
own intellectual property
around the world,

00:03:27.180 --> 00:03:31.470
is going to also license that out to
unrelated parties as one example.

00:03:31.470 --> 00:03:35.340
Another example is you
think of a CEO providing

00:03:35.340 --> 00:03:40.140
high level strategic management for
all of the operations worldwide.

00:03:40.140 --> 00:03:44.820
That person is not also providing those
services for competitors for example.

00:03:44.820 --> 00:03:48.940
So it can be very difficult to
observe an arm's length price.

00:03:48.940 --> 00:03:51.990
And that's what provides a lot
of discretion to companies,

00:03:51.990 --> 00:03:57.382
to try to establish a price
substantiated defend it.

00:03:57.382 --> 00:04:02.640
But also a price that's going to work for
best for them from a tax perspective.

00:04:02.640 --> 00:04:05.590
And this is the big question that
were seeing these days international,

00:04:05.590 --> 00:04:08.860
a fundamental question
about transfer pricing.

00:04:08.860 --> 00:04:13.980
Should arm's length be the right
standard or should it be

00:04:13.980 --> 00:04:19.700
more closely aligned with where
the physical footprint of the firm is.

00:04:19.700 --> 00:04:24.758
So discounting intellectual property a
little bit from how much it matters today,

00:04:24.758 --> 00:04:29.165
and focusing more on where sales are made,
where the customers are, and

00:04:29.165 --> 00:04:31.202
where the actual employees are.

00:04:31.202 --> 00:04:36.751
[MUSIC]

00:04:36.751 --> 00:04:38.799
It seems tempting, I think, for

00:04:38.799 --> 00:04:42.968
countries to set their tax policies
in a way to try to mitigate this on

00:04:42.968 --> 00:04:47.830
a unilateral basis where they're
acting on their own best interest.

00:04:47.830 --> 00:04:50.047
But given globalization and

00:04:50.047 --> 00:04:55.965
just the increasing amount of dealings
that are happening across borders.

00:04:55.965 --> 00:04:59.221
I don't think we get to
a long lasting answer,

00:04:59.221 --> 00:05:04.290
until we get countries cooperating
trying to harmonize tax laws.

00:05:04.290 --> 00:05:07.636
Doesn't have to be the same rate,
but at a minimum,

00:05:07.636 --> 00:05:12.508
we need to agree on how these cross-border
transactions should be priced,

00:05:12.508 --> 00:05:14.959
where profits should be recognized.

00:05:14.959 --> 00:05:18.014
And until we do that,
you're going to have companies and

00:05:18.014 --> 00:05:20.633
individuals taking
advantage of differences.

00:05:20.633 --> 00:05:26.395
[MUSIC]

00:05:26.395 --> 00:05:31.130
There's a lot of research indicating
that companies are shifting income.

00:05:31.130 --> 00:05:35.050
They are making operational and
accounting decisions to move

00:05:35.050 --> 00:05:38.637
income out of high-tax locations and
into low-tax jurisdictions.

00:05:39.920 --> 00:05:44.726
What's interesting about one of the
studies I have is that a lot of the other

00:05:44.726 --> 00:05:49.625
research out there ignores one major
tax strategy that companies can take.

00:05:49.625 --> 00:05:53.710
And that is taking advantage of
their subsidiaries with losses.

00:05:53.710 --> 00:05:57.970
Which is a really, really important
tax strategy to companies.

00:05:57.970 --> 00:06:02.760
When we include this important
strategy we find that

00:06:02.760 --> 00:06:07.390
the size of the affect is actually
much larger than we thought before.

00:06:07.390 --> 00:06:11.555
Actually all of the research out there
could underestimate the amount that

00:06:11.555 --> 00:06:16.130
corporations are engaging in this income
shifting activity by as much as 50%.

00:06:16.130 --> 00:06:18.151
Which is a very big difference.

00:06:18.151 --> 00:06:20.836
So there's a lot more of it
going on than we thought and

00:06:20.836 --> 00:06:22.637
it's a lot bigger than we thought.

00:06:22.637 --> 00:06:27.407
[MUSIC]

00:06:27.407 --> 00:06:31.312
What does it mean to pay your fair share,
whether it's a corporation or

00:06:31.312 --> 00:06:33.670
an individual, what does that look like?

00:06:33.670 --> 00:06:37.008
What does it look like to be
a good corporate citizen or

00:06:37.008 --> 00:06:40.205
a good citizen of your
country where you owe taxes.

00:06:40.205 --> 00:06:43.300
And it's a tough question.

00:06:43.300 --> 00:06:47.650
Because companies have a profit motive.

00:06:47.650 --> 00:06:51.110
They have shareholders
that they're beholden to.

00:06:51.110 --> 00:06:55.260
And there's a perspective,
the shareholder perspective of the firm,

00:06:55.260 --> 00:06:59.150
that companies should be maximizing
value for their shareholders.

00:06:59.150 --> 00:07:03.360
There's also a stakeholder perspective
which is that we're all stakeholders in

00:07:03.360 --> 00:07:07.750
this society that we've built and so
we should all pay our fair share.

00:07:07.750 --> 00:07:12.490
And I think the two perspectives aren't
necessarily mutually exclusive but

00:07:12.490 --> 00:07:15.940
it's very difficult to answer
the question what is fair because

00:07:15.940 --> 00:07:18.090
it's inherently subjective.

00:07:18.090 --> 00:07:21.580
If we know that companies
are doing this and

00:07:21.580 --> 00:07:25.720
we can estimate how much they're doing it,
and we're starting to look at some of

00:07:25.720 --> 00:07:32.000
the consequences of that is, it's natural
to ask what should the policies be.

00:07:32.000 --> 00:07:37.130
To think about what could be done to
mitigate the behavior if we think

00:07:37.130 --> 00:07:43.340
it should be mitigated or just generally
how should corporations be responding,

00:07:43.340 --> 00:07:48.200
is there a policy tool that we can
use to try to get that response,

00:07:48.200 --> 00:07:50.998
instead of what they're doing now.

00:07:50.998 --> 00:07:58.253
[MUSIC]

